This file contains comparisons 2D <--> 1D for 120Sn and 132Sn
conmputed with SV-bas and different pairing options as indicated.


We start with a doubly magic nucleus to exclude the impact of pairing
and consider 132Sn with:  HFBwidth=0 cutoff=10 closed-shell Vpair=10.
-------------------------------------------------------------------------
dim grid       E_B    E_apir  r_tot  r_prot  r_neut   T      N
 2D 0.7   -1103.20880  -0.0  4.7904  4.6524  4.8726  50.00  82.00
 2D 0.5   -1103.21470  -0.0  4.7901  4.6521  4.8723  50.00  82.00   symmetric grid
 2D 0.5   -1103.21475  -0.0  4.7901  4.6521  4.8723  50.00  82.00   asymmetric grid
 2D 0.35  -1103.21501  -0.0  4.7901  4.6521  4.8723  50.00  82.00
 1D 0.3   -1103.33749   0.0  4.7950  4.6573  4.8771
 1D 0.1   -1103.22112   0.0  4.7952  4.6574  4.8773
-------------------------------------------------------------------------
==> Grid size is not crucial in 2D, but energy is 0.1 MeV affected in 1D.
    --> the spacing of 0.3 fm is a bit to rough.
    Radii differ by 0.005 fm between 1D and 2D; the older 2D code did not
    show this problem and 1D agree with 2D radii.
    --> bug in 2D radius yet to be found!


Now we turn to 120Sn to check the impact of pairing and consider
120Sn with: width=0 cut=5  HFB V=600.
-------------------------------------------------------------------------
dim grid       E_B    E_apir  r_tot  r_prot  r_neut   T      N
 2D 0.5  -1019.76421  -11.6  4.6458  4.5704  4.6989  50.00  70.00
 2D 0.35 -1019.76303  -11.6  4.6458  4.5704  4.6989  50.00  70.00
                             4.6548  4.5794  4.7079                corrected +0.009
 1D 0.3  -1019.91871  -11.6  4.6546  4.5787  4.7080
 1D 0.1  -1019.81526  -11.6  4.6548  4.5788  4.7083
-------------------------------------------------------------------------
==> We see, again, the impact of grid spacing in 1D.
    Comparing 1D (0.1 grid) with 2D, we see a bit more different E_B
    than in 132Sn.
    --> Could that be due to the slow HFB convergence and may be
        improved by adding more iterations?
    The radii differ, again, visibly between 1D and 2D with a typical
    difference of 0.009 fm for that case.


As pairing is active, it is interesting to check also BCS for
120Sn with: width=0 cut=5  BCS V=600.
-------------------------------------------------------------------------
dim grid       E_B    E_apir  r_tot  r_prot  r_neut   T      N
 2D 0.5  -1019.63456  -11.2  4.6526  4.5737  4.7082  50.00  70.00
                             4.6586  4.5797  4.7142                corrected +0.006
 1D 0.3  -1019.74779  -11.2  4.6583  4.5796  4.7138
 1D 0.1  -1019.64448  -11.3  4.6585  4.5797  4.7140
-------------------------------------------------------------------------
==> The agreement for energy is perfect for 1D with 0.1 grid.
    The slight mismatch for the pairing energy is hard to judge
    because only 3 digits are printed.
    The difference in radii is here about 0.006 fm.

Finally a test with finite folding, again for 120Sn with:
HFBwidth=1 cut=10 HFB V=1734
-------------------------------------------------------------------------
dim grid       E_B    E_apir  r_tot  r_prot  r_neut   T      N
 2D 0.5  -1019.54098  -13.2  4.6539  4.5740  4.7102  50.00  69.95
                            4.6609  4.5810  4.7172                corrected +0.007
 1D 0.3  -1018.91042  -10.0  4.6591  4.5797  4.7150                (3800 iterations)
        -1019.97550  -14.8  4.6612  4.5802  4.7183                (10000 iterations)
 1D 0.1  -1019.87492  -14.8  4.6614  4.5804  4.7184                (10000 iterations)
-------------------------------------------------------------------------
==> There are considerable differences in E_B and pairing energy.
    Disquieting is that the result (test case 1D) depends so much
    on the iterations. Thus it may be that the mismatch 2D<-->1D
    is cause by too few 2D iterations.
    --> Yet to be checked.


Preliminary conclusions:
1. The major difference concerns radii. The 2D code must be corrected
   in that respect.
2. The situation for zero folding is already quite good conerninc energy 
   and pairing energy if high numerical precision is taken. In practice,
   we can correct less precise calculations (grid spaving!) by empirically
   derived energy correction.
3. Finite-range folding seem to be much more critical concerning
   convergence. This has yet to be studied in more detail.



